<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis IV, Duke of Modena</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1779">1779</a>–<a href="/years/1846">1846</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Francis IV of Austria-Este</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis IV (1779–1846) was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and Reggio from 1814, the first ruler of the House of Austria-Este, and from 1829 also Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a>. A conservative ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, he repressed liberal and nationalist movements. In 1831 he first encouraged the conspirator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciro-menotti">Ciro Menotti</a>, then turned against him when revolt broke out and had him executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-iv-duke-of-modena.jpg" alt="Francis IV, Duke of Modena" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adeodato Malatesta · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francesco_IV_Malatesta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
